To learn more about motors, you'd probably read about it in the Physics section of a textbook like "Electric Machines and Circuits".  There's a great open source tutorial on the Arduino site that can help you get started with this project:  https://www.arduino.cc/en/Tutorial/BasicMotor

  To control the circuit in your circuit, you'll need one of a few types of switches, and you can use an Arduino to control the switches.  The Arduino has three pins (analog input, digital input, and digital output) that is connected to the motor with resistors.  We'll call the red wire on the motor, "R1", the input from the Arduino and the red wire from the switch on the motor, "R2".  The black wire from the motor to your Arduino, and the black wire from the switch, will be connected to the Arduino's input and output.

  For controlling the mechanical action you want, you're going to use this switch to control one of your motor's phases, and to make different amounts of motion.  You want the mechanical action of the motor to be continuous and have linear and sinusoidal motion.  The switch will control which phase of the motor current flows, and thus which one of the motor's moving parts you'll see act.  Different phases are created by different voltage levels, and the voltage can be applied by a transistor with a resistor in series with a voltage source, like a 9V battery, or directly by a switch or an LED.  You could connect an LED to a transistor voltage source (or use an extra battery or other device to do it by yourself!) and leave the resistor connected, or you could connect the resistor to any one or more of the outputs to form the switch to control a one-to-one voltage that applies to the motor and the moving parts.  Or you could connect the voltage to the switch, so the switch applies to only one motor phase at a time, and you'd instead need a transistor to control one or more of the motor phases.

